\paragraph{The residual time-scale barrier.} Let \(H\) be self-adjoint and let a differentiable approximate state \(\Psi_a(t)\) lie in its domain, with residual \(R(t)=(i\partial_t-H)\Psi_a(t)\). If the exact state has the same initial value, differentiation of \(e^{itH}\Psi_a(t)\) and integration give
\[
 \|\Psi(t)-\Psi_a(t)\|\le\int_0^{|t|}\|R(s)\|\,ds
\]
with the corresponding time orientation for negative \(t\). Thus a bound \(\|R\|\le C\alpha^{-p}\) only yields \(C|t|\alpha^{-p}\). It tends to zero on \(|t|\le\alpha^{p-\delta}\), \(\delta>0\), but yields no vanishing control at \(\alpha^p\) or later.
\paragraph{A cancellation that would be needed.} If in the interaction representation a residual has form \(e^{i\omega t}B(t)\), with \(B\) norm differentiable, integration by parts gives
\[
 \left\|\int_0^t e^{i\omega s}B(s)ds\right\|
 \le\frac{2\sup\|B\|+\int_0^t\|B'(s)\|ds}{|\omega|},\qquad\omega\ne0.
\]
This shows precisely how oscillatory cancellation can beat direct residual integration, and why a small or resonant frequency defeats that route.
\paragraph{Source comparison.} No residual exponent or nonresonance condition is claimed for the actual Landau--Pekar ansatz. The first gap is extracting such cancellation or constructing a controlled correction in the source norm, uniformly beyond the \(\alpha^2\) window. Formal ansatz extension alone does not do it. Initial-state assumptions, phonon fluctuations and the source time normalization remain to be addressed.

